COMMAND NAME: gpaddpart

Adds new child table partitions to a parent table that was 
previously partitioned using gpcreatepart.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpaddpart -t <schema.parent_table> -n <add_child_count> [-c | -C] 
[-I] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -t <schema.parent_table> -s <new_start_value> [-c | -C] 
[-I] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -t <schema.parent_table> -o <new_list_value>[,<value>,...] 
[-c | -C] [-I] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] 
[-D] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpaddpart -? | -v


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpaddpart script adds additional child tables to a parent table 
that has already been partitioned using gpcreatepart. This is useful 
if you need to create additional child table partitions for newly 
added data. It will use the same partitioning scheme (date range, 
numerical range, or list) as was originally defined when the parent 
table was first partitioned. It performs the following tasks:

* Creates new child tables that inherit all attributes from the 
  parent table (columns, constraints, table permissions, etc.), as 
  well as the original partitioning attributes and Greenplum 
  distribution key. The child table names are created using a 
  predefined naming convention of:
    
    <top_level_parent>_c_d_<child_depth_#>_<partition_type>_<partition_number>_<oid_of_parent_table>_<partition_criteria>

  For example:
    sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* Defines CHECK constraints on the child tables based on the new 
  date range, numeric range, or list value specified.

* If the existing child tables have rewrite rules, it will create 
  the appropriate rewrite rules on the parent table so that INSERT 
  statements are automatically redirected to the correct child table. 
  The rewrite rules are created using a predefined naming convention of:

     rule_<child_table_name>

  For example:
     rule_sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* If the existing child tables have indexes, it will create the 
  corresponding indexes on the new child tables. Indexes are created 
  with a predefined naming convention of:
 
     idx_<column_number>_<child_table_name>

  For example:
    idx_1_sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* If the parent table contains data, using the -c option will 
  empty the parent table into a temporary table and re-insert 
  records into the parent table, thereby triggering the rewrite 
  rules to route the records to the appropriate child tables. The 
  -C option will not route the records through the parent table, but 
  instead insert them directly into the child tables based on the 
  child table CHECK constraints defined. The -C option offers better 
  performance, but should only be used if every record can be mapped 
  to a child table CHECK constraint. -C will rollback if there are any 
  leftover records that do not map to a child table, leaving all 
  records in the parent table.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <schema.parent_table>

Required. The schema-qualified name of the parent table that for 
which you want to create new partitioned child tables.


-s <new_start_value>

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies the numeric 
value to use as the starting point for creating additional 
child tables.


-n <new_children_count>

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies the number of 
additional year, month, day, or hour child tables to create from 
where the partitioning scheme last left off.


-o <new_list_values>

Required for list partitioning. A comma separated list of values 
for which to create new list partitioned child tables. Make sure 
there are no spaces between list values and comma separators. To add a 
null check constraint add the text NULL to the list.


-c (move data to child table via rewrite rules)

Moves data from the parent table into the newly created child 
tables by moving data from the parent table into a temporary table 
and then re-inserting to the parent table to trigger the rewrite 
rules. The default behavior is to leave records in the parent 
table.


-C (move data to child table via direct insert)

Moves data from the parent table into the newly created child 
tables by moving data from the parent table into a temporary 
table and then inserting directly into the child tables based on 
the CHECK constraints defined. This option offers better performance 
than -c, but should only be used if every record can be mapped to 
a child table CHECK constraint. The move will roll back if there 
are any leftover records that do not map to a child table, leaving 
all records in the parent table.


-I (do not create indexes on new child tables)

Do not create any indexes on the newly added child tables. Use this 
option if you plan to load the child tables directly. Creating the 
indexes after loading will improve load performance.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of parallel insert processes to run. The default is 4.


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l <log_directory>

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By 
default log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output to debug level.


-x <database_name>

Optional. The name of the database where the parent table 
resides. If not supplied, the script uses the default database 
(which is the most recently created database).


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, the 
value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Add new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is 
currently range partitioned by salary. You want to increase 
the top salary range from what it currently is to $1,000,000.

gpaddpart -t hr.employee -s 10000000


Add new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is 
currently list partitioned by region. You want to add a new 
region for china and india.

gpaddpart -t dw.sales -o china,india


Add two new partitioned child tables to a parent table that is 
currently date partitioned by month.

gpaddpart -t dw.sales -n 2


